title: CHEK2 mutation: implications and management
type: text/vnd.tiddlywiki

* ''Interpretation:'' Confirm a germline pathogenic/likely pathogenic variant; a variant of uncertain significance does not change management. CHEK2 confers moderate, variant-dependent cancer susceptibility, not Li-Fraumeni syndrome.
* ''Breast risk:'' Female lifetime risk is approximately 23%–27% for actionable variants; family history modifies risk. Ile157Thr, Ser428Phe, Thr476Met, and Glu64Lys do not independently justify intensified screening.
* ''Screening:'' Annual mammography from 40; consider annual contrast-enhanced breast MRI from 30–35, individualized by family history and risk. Continue surveillance of remaining breast tissue after cancer treatment.
* ''Risk reduction:'' Preventive bilateral or contralateral mastectomy is not routine; individualize using family history, estimated risk, and preferences. No established ovarian association: no CHEK2-driven salpingo-oophorectomy.
* ''Other organs:'' Consider annual prostate-specific antigen testing from 40 through shared decision-making. Use population colorectal screening unless personal/family history warrants more; older CHEK2-based colonoscopy schedules are superseded. No routine additional thyroid, renal, or pancreatic surveillance solely for CHEK2.
* ''Treatment pearl:'' Treat breast cancer by standard stage/subtype criteria; CHEK2 alone does not establish PARP-inhibitor sensitivity.
* ''Family:'' Refer for genetic counseling and offer adult relatives targeted testing; each child of a heterozygous carrier has a 50% inheritance risk.

Sources: NCCN Genetic/Familial High-Risk Assessment: Breast, Ovarian, Pancreatic, and Prostate v1.2027, GENE-A 8/19, 19/19; [[ACMG 2023|https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gim.2023.100870]], pp. 13–15; [[GeneReviews 2026|https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK615090/]], Surveillance/Genetic Counseling; ASCO SEP 2026, ch. 6, Table 6-1B, p. 268 (PDF 275).
